      "title": "Post-Exploitation: Abusing Chrome's debugging feature to observe and control browsing sessions remotely",
      "summary": "Chrome’s remote debugging feature enables malware post-exploitation to gain access to cookies. Root privileges are not required. This is a pretty well-known and commonly used adversarial technique - at least since 2018 when Cookie Crimes was released. However, remote debugging also allows observing user activities and sensitive personal information (aka spying on users) and controlling the browser from a remote computer . Below screenshot shows a simulated attacker controlling the victim’s browser and navigating to chrome://settings to inspect information:",
